    "# **Poster**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2687fa86",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Introduction:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Setup**: One effusion probe is trained once on 70 % of the clear effusion cases and then frozen. The reamining 30 % reports are edited, reembedded with the same BioclinicalBERT. The probe is then use to score the new embeddings and compare against baseline.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **The edits**: \n",
    "    - Three edits are applied to the test set\n",
    "    - Negation: replace effusion senetences with \"no pleural effusion\". Hypothesis is this should decrease P(effusion) and potentially flip some cases\n",
    "    - Size swaps: \"small\" is swapped with \"large\" and vice versa. Hypothesis: This probabilites should remain more or less the same, since the presence of effusion is not changed.\n",
    "    - Insertion: Insert \"Bibasilar atelectasis\" into cases with no effusion. Hypothesis: This should increase probability of effusion since Bibasilar atelectasis is often mentioned together with effusion. The resuslts are presented in the table below."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9da71241",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation**\n",
    "- *Were the hypotheses confirmed?*\n",
    "    - Ngeation: Partly confirmed. P(effusion) droppped on average ( 0.94 -> 0.52). The embeddings do react to \"no effusion\". But only 43 % of the reports flipped below 0.5. The low cosine shift indicates the pooling of the reports into one embedding is affected by more than just the presence of effusion in the description.  \n",
    "\n",
    "    - Size swap: P stayed the same (0.95 -> 0.95) and no report flipped. The model treats size as a change in the description and not in the presence of effusion \n",
    "\n",
    "    - Insertion: P(effusion) increased ( 0.36 -> 0.54) with 17 % of the reports flipped to effusion. Atelectasis occurs with effusion frequently enough to have an impact that pushes the prediction towards effusion without expliciutly mentioning effusion itself.\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  }
